This version replaces the 64K-entry lookup table with a 1024-entry table for message numbers below 1024. Message numbers of 1024 or higher fall back to a linear scan of `mesgs`.

- **Why it should be faster:** the full table is 64 KB with 8-bit indices or 128 KB with 16-bit ones, so lookups tend to miss the cache. The small table is about 2 KB and stays cache-resident.
- **Why it returns the same message as before:**
  - Every table slot keeps the lowest index `i` with that `num`, because the build loop runs backwards and lower indices overwrite higher ones.
  - The fallback scan runs forwards from index 0, so it also returns the lowest matching index.
  - When nothing matches, both paths return `NULL`.
- **Expected cost of the fallback:** profile message numbers are almost all small, so the scan should rarely run. I have not checked the profile for numbers of 1024 or above, and these cost a full scan of `mesgs`.

```cpp
const Profile::MESG* Profile::GetMesg(const FIT_UINT16 num)
{
    enum { SMALL = 1024 };
    struct Index
    {
        FIT_UINT16 idx[SMALL];
        Index()
        {
            for (int k = 0; k < SMALL; k++)
                idx[k] = 0xFFFF;
            for (int i = MESGS - 1; i >= 0; i--)
            {
                const FIT_UINT16 n = (FIT_UINT16)mesgs[i].num;
                if (n < SMALL)
                    idx[n] = (FIT_UINT16)i;
            }
        }
    };
    static const Index table;

    if (num < SMALL)
    {
        const FIT_UINT16 i = table.idx[num];
        if (i == 0xFFFF)
            return NULL;
        return &mesgs[i];
    }

    for (int i = 0; i < MESGS; i++)
    {
        if ((FIT_UINT16)mesgs[i].num == num)
            return &mesgs[i];
    }
    return NULL;
}
```